Throttle rendering updates while the page is loading (WEBKIT_LOAD_RENDERING_INTERVAL_MS).

Paint-storm root cause, finally pinned with the WEBKIT_PAINT_LOG diagnostic
plus a breakpoint on GraphicsLayerCoordinated::setNeedsDisplayInRect: a
franceinfo.fr load issues ~8900 renderer-level repaint rects, all through
RenderElement::repaintAfterLayoutIfNeeded - i.e. each of the dozens of
style/layout passes during load (stylesheets, module scripts, images, ads
landing) legitimately repaints swaths of elements (block flows ~3.2k, layer
position updates ~2.4k, flex items ~1k, SVG icons ~1.1k, images ~0.4k).
The invalidations are correct and precisely sized; the problem is CADENCE:
the same visible tiles get re-rastered ~100x over one load (396 tile-paint
passes, ~340 Mpx = 130 screenfuls, on a page whose scrolled contents layer
is 95000 px tall with 23000-px-wide carousel strips).

Whole-layer invalidations were ruled out first: with the
no-full-repaint-on-layer-grow patch, one load shows GLC FULL x4 (tiny
layers), zero DROP-ALL, zero scale changes - yet paint volume was unchanged.

So batch the passes instead of fighting the invalidations: while the main
load is in progress (ProgressTracker originating-frame window), cap the
page's rendering-update cadence to WEBKIT_LOAD_RENDERING_INTERVAL_MS
(e.g. 250 = 4 Hz). Style recalc, layout, intersection observers, rAF and
the layer flush all ride the rendering update, so dirty regions accumulate
and each batched update paints the union once instead of once per landing
resource. Invalidation correctness is untouched; JS-forced sync layouts
are unaffected (they don't paint). The throttle routes through the
existing ThrottlingReason machinery endpoints
(Page::preferredRenderingUpdateFramesPerSecond / Interval + 
RenderingUpdateScheduler::adjustRenderingUpdateFrequency +
renderingUpdateFramesPerSecondChanged), which WPE honours end-to-end via
WebDisplayRefreshMonitor -> UIProcess DisplayLink per-observer preferred
FPS. While throttled, animation frame rates do not raise the cadence back
up (same rule as the stock throttling reasons).

Trade-offs: progressive rendering during load updates at the capped rate
(content appears in ~250ms steps instead of continuously), and rAF
animations run at the capped rate until the load event - both only while
the progress tracker reports an active main load. Unset/0 = off.

